  multi={false}
/>. And read 
<Navigable 
  content={"this one"}
  href={"https://dave.cheney.net/2016/04/07/constant-errors"}
  external={true}
  row={4}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/> too while you're at it. This article is really just me sharing these resources, and I won't say much as I believe they explain it better than I could.
</p>

TL;DR:

> Treat all errors as opaque. If not possible, assert errors for behavior. Use `errors.Wrap` and `errors.Cause`.

<p>
These functions aren't natively in Go though, it comes from another package, 
<Navigable 
  content={"github.com/pkg/errors"}
  href={"https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/pkg/errors"}
  external={true}
  row={5}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/>. It's not a big deal, but maybe it's not your cup of tea.
</p>

<p>
This was written way back in 2016 though. It's been 9 years, and while I'm not yet super familiar with the language, I'm sure it has evolved since then. JetBrains wrote this year about more 
<Navigable 
  content={"modern practices"}
  href={"https://blog.jetbrains.com/go/2026/03/02/secure-go-error-handling-best-practices/"}
  external={true}
  row={6}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/>, which still uses opaque wrapping.
</p>

For now I'll just use what the standard library provides, but it's good to keep the other approach at the back of my head.